WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:10.450
So I need to warn you -- fair warning, I am
new to this practice of talking about social

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:12.059
policy.

00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:17.340
That's because until recently, I was engaged
in implementing social policy.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:23.599
As the commissioner of New York City's largest
public social services agency, I was responsible

00:00:23.599 --> 00:00:30.580
for the day-to-day operation of some of our
nation's biggest antipoverty programs.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:36.170
At the New York City Human Resources Administration,
we administered everything from cash, welfare,

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:42.440
to food stamp benefits, to Medicaid eligibility,
to child support collections, and a host of

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.920
other programs in between.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:51.579
So my perspective comes from someone who's
seen these programs work day-to-day over a

00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:54.769
long period of time.

00:00:54.769 --> 00:01:01.719
And what I learned and what I saw was that
government can create an environment whereby

00:01:01.719 --> 00:01:07.370
people in poverty can move into work and raise
their incomes.

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:11.730
But it takes adherence to certain core principles.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:20.710
And those principles are: we must require
work in return for assistance.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:27.290
We must also reward work by making low wages
go farther.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:35.320
We must always talk about two-parent families
and the importance of mothers and fathers

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:38.780
in the appropriate raising of children.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:47.230
And finally, we must be vigilant about promoting
a job-producing, growing economy -- low wage

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:54.180
jobs, middle wage jobs, high wage jobs, providing
opportunity to low income Americans.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:02:01.810
Now, I suppose it's possible that you might
be thinking how did I, how did someone like

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:06.890
me get into the poverty fighting world to
begin with.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.899
I mean, look at me.

00:02:09.899 --> 00:02:15.360
Frankly, I look kind of Republican even, even
conservative.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.640
And I am.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:23.499
But I chose to make my career helping struggling
Americans because of what I saw in my father's

00:02:23.499 --> 00:02:29.519
career working in the Civil Rights Division
of the United States Justice Department.

00:02:29.519 --> 00:02:34.480
He came to Washington on 1960, at the end
of the Eisenhower administration and joined

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:39.530
the Civil Rights Division at that point and
stayed through President Kennedy's administration

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.799
and almost to the end of the Johnson administration.

00:02:42.799 --> 00:02:49.319
And during that time, he was engaged or involved
in all of the really difficult problems and

00:02:49.319 --> 00:02:51.780
conflicts that occurred during that time.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:58.090
And it was positive and forward looking and
it made our country better.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:05.230
And ultimately, it was inspiring because it
showed that government and us, Americans,

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:07.939
could improve our country.

00:03:07.939 --> 00:03:13.180
And then, at the request of his friend, Robert
Kennedy, we moved to New York City, where

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:18.739
Robert Kennedy had started a small antipoverty
program in Bedford-Stuyvesant, one of the

00:03:18.739 --> 00:03:27.430
largest slums in America at the time, a neighborhood
of 500,000 New Yorkers in central Brooklyn.

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:33.340
And my father was involved in a small program
bringing economic development and private

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.120
sector investment and some real estate development.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:46.879
And over his experience there, they made some
small gains for a few people on a few blocks

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:48.459
in Brooklyn.

00:03:48.459 --> 00:03:53.629
But meanwhile, larger forces were at work.

00:03:53.629 --> 00:04:00.110
Larger forces were at work, and they were
the welfare entitlement state that had said,

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:04.689
as part of the reforms that took place in
the '60s and '70s, we will provide assistance

00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:12.559
to people without regard to what they do -- as
a matter of right or a matter of entitlement.

00:04:12.559 --> 00:04:17.380
So while a few people were benefited by the
Bedford-Stuyvesant Restoration Corporation,

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:23.569
hundreds of thousands were sucked into a life
of dependency and no work.

00:04:23.569 --> 00:04:27.289
And our city was almost brought to its knees.

00:04:27.289 --> 00:04:33.780
So when I had a chance to go to work in the
government, I had those two things in my mind:

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:38.479
success and civil rights, not really success
in poverty.

00:04:38.479 --> 00:04:45.920
And so I joined the Pataki administration
in 1995, luckily right at the beginning of

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:49.389
the welfare reform movement in America.

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:56.430
Governor Engler, Governor Thompson, Bill Clinton,
Newt Gingrich decided to change welfare as

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.789
we know it.

00:04:58.789 --> 00:05:03.879
And I worked there for Governor Pataki and
then for Mayor Giuliani for that entire period.

00:05:03.879 --> 00:05:07.360
And what did we do?

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.620
We required work in return for public assistance.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:16.580
We made it a clear expectation that it was
a reciprocal relationship between the receiver

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:22.440
of assistance and the provider of aid.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:25.009
In that regard, we changed the names of our
offices.

00:05:25.009 --> 00:05:28.620
They used to be called -- think of this, the
welfare offices in New York City used to be

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:31.350
called Income Maintenance Centers.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:33.180
Income Maintenance Centers.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:36.479
We changed them to be Job Centers.

00:05:36.479 --> 00:05:41.640
We paid people that tried to help people get
jobs only if they got jobs.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.949
We paid for performance.

00:05:43.949 --> 00:05:45.800
We talked about child support enforcement.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:50.849
We said children need two parents contributing.

00:05:50.849 --> 00:05:57.370
There's a responsibility for both parents
to care for their children.

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:02.861
And we created an environment where if there
wasn't a job or someone couldn't work, we

00:06:02.861 --> 00:06:08.150
said to them, well, we need you to be involved
in the schedule and rigor on a day-to-day

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:11.150
basis of something that looks like a job.

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:16.860
We'll provide assistance, but we need you
to be in a circumstance where you're mirroring

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:21.879
the schedule and rigor of employment.

00:06:21.879 --> 00:06:25.090
So that's the tough stuff.

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:28.310
We also rewarded work.

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:34.660
So public health insurance for low wage workers,
we were generous with it.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:39.479
Food Stamp benefits for working people, we
provided it.

00:06:39.479 --> 00:06:44.800
Childcare assistance, the Earned Income Tax
Credit -- we promoted these supports for low

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:48.780
wage workers, so wages could go further.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:50.740
We talked about family.

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:52.580
We promoted two-parent families.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:07:01.889
And always, always we understood that in order
to succeed at its best we needed a thriving

00:07:01.889 --> 00:07:03.780
job producing economy.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:10.639
And we didn't make any disparagements about
dead-end jobs or low wage jobs.

00:07:10.639 --> 00:07:12.650
We said we need jobs.

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:14.189
Any job is a step up.

00:07:14.189 --> 00:07:18.419
So we promoted hospitality and restaurants
and health care.

00:07:18.419 --> 00:07:25.810
And every time I saw those economic development
guys in the halls of government, I said, make

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:28.680
it happen.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:29.680
We need jobs.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:36.300
Require work, reward work, talk about family,
promote a strong economy.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:40.210
Those are the four principles of success in
this endeavor.

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:42.020
So what happened?

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:43.349
Where were the statistics?

00:07:43.349 --> 00:07:47.780
So everyone knows welfare caseloads plummeted.

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:53.370
The peak welfare caseload in New York City
was 1.1 million cash welfare recipients.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:58.380
One in seven New Yorkers was receiving the
dole.

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:04.450
Recently, last year, before I left, we were
down to 350,000 out of a city of more than

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:05.650
8 million.

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:09.409
Tremendous decline, but that wasn't our greatest
achievement.

00:08:09.409 --> 00:08:14.699
Our greatest achievement was work rates for
never-married mothers.

00:08:14.699 --> 00:08:17.410
Work rates for never-married mothers.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:24.099
The very people who were on cash welfare in
the first place, the very people who the program

00:08:24.099 --> 00:08:26.499
was originally set up to help.

00:08:26.499 --> 00:08:34.340
It went from 43 percent to 63 percent -- a
20 percent point increase, unprecedented and

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:40.410
remarkable and it happened all across the
country, not only in New York City.

00:08:40.410 --> 00:08:49.720
Child poverty in New York City declined dramatically
in the late '90s up into the 2000s, from 43

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:55.710
percent to 28 percent -- significant decline
in child poverty.

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:58.660
And what was the most compelling statistic
of all?

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:01.110
And this is a national statistic.

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:10.251
African-American child poverty in the United
States dropped from 44 percent in 1994 to

00:09:10.251 --> 00:09:13.790
30 percent in 2008 -- 2001, excuse me.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:15.500
Now, think of that.

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:20.360
Almost half African-American children were
in poverty before welfare reform, less than

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:23.750
a third after welfare reform.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:32.820
Now, since the early 2000s, I have to say,
I'm not sure we've kept focused on those core

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:33.960
principles.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:37.820
We've gotten away from the required work requirements.

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:43.910
We've made rewarding work sometimes replace
work, so that some of the benefits we provide

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:46.860
provide disincentives to people being in work.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:51.880
We haven't talked about the importance of
two-parent families enough.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:58.779
And most egregiously, we've allowed our economy
to not grow.

00:09:58.779 --> 00:10:05.940
The greatest tragedy of the anemic recovery
we have been through these last five years

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:10.730
is the damage it's done to poor Americans.

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:17.170
So from someone who's been in the business,
who's seen it work successfully and seen it

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:27.140
maybe not work so successfully, require work,
reward work, talk about family, promote a

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:29.019
strong economy.

00:10:29.019 --> 00:10:30.019
Those are the keys.

00:10:30.019 --> 00:10:35.260
Now, I have one last point I want to make
and this is an important one.

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:46.940
It is a common desire to promise an outcome,
to guarantee that we will -- if we do this,

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:55.660
we will guarantee people's rising safely into
the middle class.

00:10:55.660 --> 00:10:57.880
Governments can't promise that.

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:00.760
They can't guarantee that.

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:03.350
People make that happen.

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:10.370
People make that happen if the environment
is created for that to happen.

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:15.779
So to promise an outcome of that kind, to
guarantee it as if it would -- as if government

00:11:15.779 --> 00:11:20.820
could provide it, it's a false promise and
it's a harmful promise.

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:28.769
But if we create an environment whereby people
in poverty can move into work and increase

00:11:28.769 --> 00:11:29.770
their incomes.

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:32.120
You know what?

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:33.240
They'll do it.

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:35.370
They did it before.

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:43.130
They proved all of the commentators and all
of the prognosticators wrong about what would

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:48.740
happen if we went to a work-based welfare
program.

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:50.730
They were the heroes of welfare reform.

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:57.600
And we need to get back to that kind of policy
to help poor Americans in the future.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:00.920
Thank you very much.

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:02.170
Thanks for watching.

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:07.190
We'd like to know what you think about fighting
poverty, so tell us in the comments below.

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:10.690
Then please help us spread the word by sharing
this video.

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:16.170
For more vision talks click the subscribe
button and visit the website ThePursuitOfHappiness.com.

